('causal', 8, 'Module 8: Difference-in-Differences',
  'The canonical 2x2, parallel trends and pre-trend plots, and the staggered-adoption problem with the Goodman-Bacon decomposition and modern estimators.',
  $html$<h2>Module 8: Difference-in-Differences</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">When a programme rolls out to some places (or people) and not others, and you observe outcomes before and after, difference-in-differences (DiD) uses the untreated group's change over time as the counterfactual for the treated group's change. It is the workhorse of policy evaluation &mdash; and the design where a recent wave of research has overturned the default way practitioners ran it.</p></div>

<h3>The 2&times;2 Logic</h3>
<p>With one treated group, one comparison group, and two periods, the estimator double-differences:</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ \widehat{\tau}_{\text{DiD}} \;=\; \big(\bar{Y}^{\,\text{post}}_{T} - \bar{Y}^{\,\text{pre}}_{T}\big) \;-\; \big(\bar{Y}^{\,\text{post}}_{C} - \bar{Y}^{\,\text{pre}}_{C}\big) \]</div><div class="equation-caption">The first difference removes anything fixed about the treated group; the second removes common time shocks. What survives is the treatment effect &mdash; <em>if</em> the two groups would have trended together.</div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Parallel trends &mdash; the whole assumption</div><p>DiD identifies the ATT only if, <strong>absent treatment, the treated group's outcome would have followed the same trend as the control group's</strong>. This is untestable (it concerns a counterfactual trend), but you can build confidence by checking that <em>pre-treatment</em> trends were parallel.</p></div>
<div class="dag-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 420 200" role="img" aria-label="Parallel-trends plot: treated and control trend together pre-period; treated jumps after treatment; dashed counterfactual continues parallel" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><line x1="45" y1="175" x2="400" y2="175" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="45" y1="20" x2="45" y2="175" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="225" y1="20" x2="225" y2="175" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="225" y="193" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">treatment</text><line x1="70" y1="120" x2="225" y2="90" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="225" y1="90" x2="380" y2="45" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="386" y="45" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">T</text><line x1="70" y1="150" x2="225" y2="120" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="225" y1="120" x2="380" y2="105" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="386" y="108" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">C</text><line x1="225" y1="90" x2="380" y2="75" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="332" y="70" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">counterfactual</text><line x1="380" y1="75" x2="380" y2="45" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text x="392" y="62" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">&tau;</text></svg><div class="dag-caption">Treated (T) and control (C) trend in parallel before treatment. The dashed line extends the control's trend onto the treated group as its counterfactual; the gap after treatment is the ATT.</div></div>

<h3>The Regression Form &mdash; and Its Modern Caveat</h3>
<p>With many units and periods, DiD is run as two-way fixed effects (TWFE):</p>
<div class="equation-box"><div class="equation">\[ Y_{it} \;=\; \alpha_i \;+\; \lambda_t \;+\; \beta\,D_{it} \;+\; \varepsilon_{it} \]</div><div class="equation-caption">\(\alpha_i\) absorbs fixed unit differences, \(\lambda_t\) common time shocks; \(\beta\) is the DiD estimate. With a single treatment date this is fine.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Staggered rollouts break plain TWFE.</strong> When units are treated at <em>different</em> times and effects vary, TWFE uses already-treated units as controls for later-treated ones, so &ldquo;forbidden comparisons&rdquo; can flip the sign (Goodman-Bacon 2021; de Chaisemartin &amp; D'Haultf&oelig;uille 2020). Use a modern estimator &mdash; Callaway &amp; Sant'Anna (2021), Sun &amp; Abraham (2021), or the <code>did</code> imputation approach &mdash; and report an event study.</p></div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2"/></svg> Worked example &mdash; a state rolls out a maternity-benefit scheme</div>
<p>One state introduces a cash maternity benefit; a neighbouring state does not. Institutional-delivery rates (%) before and after:</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Pre</th><th>Post</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Treated state</td><td>62</td><td>78</td><td>+16</td></tr><tr><td>Comparison state</td><td>60</td><td>69</td><td>+9</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Difference-in-differences</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>+7 pp</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Both states improved (a national time trend); the scheme's effect is the <em>extra</em> 7 pp the treated state gained &mdash; valid only if, without the scheme, it would have risen by the same 9 pp as its neighbour.</p></div>

<h3>In Code</h3>
<pre><code>## R — TWFE for a single date; Callaway–Sant'Anna for staggered rollout
library(fixest)
feols(inst_delivery ~ treat | district + year, cluster = ~district, data = d)
## event study (leads/lags relative to treatment year):
feols(inst_delivery ~ i(rel_year, ref = -1) | district + year, data = d)
## robust to staggered timing + heterogeneous effects:
library(did)
att &lt;- att_gt(yname="inst_delivery", tname="year", idname="district",
              gname="first_treat_year", data = d)
aggte(att, type = "dynamic")     # event-study ATT, bias-free under staggering</code></pre>
<pre><code>* Stata — TWFE, event study, and a heterogeneity-robust estimator
reghdfe inst_delivery treat, absorb(district year) vce(cluster district)
csdid inst_delivery, ivar(district) time(year) gvar(first_treat_year)
estat event        // dynamic ATT / pre-trend leads</code></pre>

<div class="problem-set"><div class="problem-set-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5H7a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2h-2M9 5a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2M9 5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2m-3 7h3m-3 4h3m-6-4h.01M9 16h.01"/></svg> Practice</div>
<ol>
<li>An event-study plot shows the treated and control groups already diverging two years <em>before</em> the policy. What does this imply for the DiD estimate?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">A pre-trend means parallel trends is doubtful: the groups were on different trajectories before treatment, so the post-period gap mixes the policy with that ongoing divergence. The DiD estimate is likely biased. Options: find a better comparison group, allow group-specific trends (cautiously), or abandon DiD here.</div></details></li>
<li>Why can two-way fixed effects give a <em>negative</em> estimate even when the treatment helped everyone, in a staggered rollout?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">TWFE is a weighted average of all 2&times;2 comparisons, including ones that use <em>already-treated</em> units as controls for later-treated units. If effects grow over time, those forbidden comparisons subtract a large already-treated change, contributing negative weights that can dominate and flip the sign. Heterogeneity-robust estimators avoid the forbidden comparisons.</div></details></li>
<li>A reviewer asks you to &ldquo;prove parallel trends.&rdquo; What can and cannot be shown?
<details><summary>Solution</summary><div class="solution">You cannot prove it &mdash; it is a statement about an unobserved counterfactual trend. You can provide supporting evidence: parallel <em>pre-treatment</em> trends (event-study leads near zero), robustness to alternative comparison groups, and placebo tests. State it as an assumption made credible, not proven.</div></details></li>
</ol></div>

<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">If your evaluation rests on DiD, draw the event-study plot first and put it on slide one. Funders and sceptics believe a clean pre-trend far more than a single coefficient &mdash; and if the pre-trend is ugly, you want to know before you build the whole report on it.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a></div></div></div>

<div class="source-citation">Sources: Card &amp; Krueger (1994), <em>AER</em> 84(4); Goodman-Bacon (2021), <em>J. Econometrics</em> 225(2); Callaway &amp; Sant'Anna (2021), <em>J. Econometrics</em> 225(2); de Chaisemartin &amp; D'Haultf&oelig;uille (2020), <em>AER</em> 110(9); Sun &amp; Abraham (2021), <em>J. Econometrics</em> 225(2); Roth, Sant'Anna, Bilinski &amp; Poe (2023), <em>J. Econometrics</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304407621001445" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Goodman-Bacon (2021). &ldquo;Difference-in-Differences with Variation in Treatment Timing.&rdquo;</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="https://bcallaway11.github.io/did/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>Callaway &amp; Sant'Anna (2021). <em>did</em> package &amp; paper.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm the assumption and the staggered-timing fix.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The identifying assumption of difference-in-differences is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1a" value="a"><label for="m8q1a">Random assignment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1b" value="b"><label for="m8q1b">Parallel trends: the groups would have moved together absent treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1c" value="c"><label for="m8q1c">A strong first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1d" value="d"><label for="m8q1d">No manipulation at a cutoff.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> With staggered treatment timing, a plain two-way fixed effects estimate can be biased because it: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2a" value="a"><label for="m8q2a">Uses already-treated units as controls and can apply negative weights.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2b" value="b"><label for="m8q2b">Has too few time periods.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2c" value="c"><label for="m8q2c">Ignores the running variable.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2d" value="d"><label for="m8q2d">Requires randomisation.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Similar pre-trends between groups are: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3a" value="a"><label for="m8q3a">Proof that parallel trends holds.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3b" value="b"><label for="m8q3b">Irrelevant to the design.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3c" value="c"><label for="m8q3c">Supportive evidence, not proof, of parallel trends.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3d" value="d"><label for="m8q3d">A sign of a weak instrument.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Think of a policy that rolled out across states or districts at different times. Which units would serve as the comparison early on, and why might a plain two-way fixed effects model mislead you here?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 9, 'Module 9: Synthetic Control',
  'Constructing a counterfactual from a weighted donor pool for single-unit interventions, the centrality of pre-period fit, and placebo-based inference.',
  $html$<h2>Module 9: Synthetic Control</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Sometimes only one unit is treated: one state passes a reform, one city runs a programme. With a single treated unit and no obvious comparison, synthetic control builds one from a weighted blend of untreated units that tracks the treated unit before the intervention.</p></div>
<h3>Building a Counterfactual from Donors</h3>
<p>Choose a donor pool of untreated units. Find weights on those donors so the weighted average matches the treated unit’s pre-intervention outcome path and predictors. After the intervention, the gap between the treated unit and its synthetic version is the estimated effect. Abadie and Gardeazabal (2003) introduced it for the economic cost of conflict in the Basque Country; Abadie, Diamond and Hainmueller (2010) formalised it.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> What Makes It Credible</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Pre-period fit</div><div class="definition-text">The synthetic unit must track the treated unit closely for many pre-intervention periods. A poor pre-fit makes the post-period gap meaningless.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Donor pool discipline</div><div class="definition-text">Donors must be plausibly unaffected by the treatment and not subject to their own shocks at the same time.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Placebo inference</div><div class="definition-text">Re-run the method pretending each donor was treated. If the real treated unit’s gap is extreme relative to the placebo gaps, the effect is unlikely to be chance (Abadie 2021).</div></div></div>
<p>A natural Indian application is a single-state policy shock, such as Bihar’s 2016 alcohol prohibition, where a synthetic Bihar built from other states provides the counterfactual for outcomes like consumption, crime, or household spending.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>Inference is by placebo, not the usual standard error.</strong> With one treated unit there is no sampling distribution in the ordinary sense. Credibility comes from the pre-period fit and from showing the treated gap stands out against placebo runs on the donors.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>A good post-period gap with a poor pre-period fit is worthless.</strong> Always show the pre-intervention tracking plot. If the synthetic unit cannot reproduce the treated unit’s history, it cannot stand in for its counterfactual future.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">Synthetic control sells itself in a single chart: the real line and the synthetic line on top of each other before the policy, then splitting after. When the pre-period fit is genuinely tight, it is the most persuasive quasi-experimental picture I know for a single-unit reform.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: synthetic control with placebo inference
library(tidysynth)
sc &lt;- df %&gt;% synthetic_control(outcome = y, unit = state, time = year,
        i_unit = "Bihar", i_time = 2016) %&gt;%
        generate_predictor(...) %&gt;% generate_weights() %&gt;% generate_control()
plot_trends(sc); plot_placebos(sc)</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Abadie &amp; Gardeazabal (2003), <em>AER</em> 93(1); Abadie, Diamond &amp; Hainmueller (2010), <em>JASA</em> 105(490); Abadie (2021), <em>JEL</em> 59(2).</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Abadie, Diamond &amp; Hainmueller (2010). "Synthetic Control Methods for Comparative Case Studies."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Abadie (2021). "Using Synthetic Controls: Feasibility, Data Requirements, and Methodology." <em>JEL</em> 59(2).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Abadie &amp; Gardeazabal (2003). "The Economic Costs of Conflict." <em>AER</em> 93(1).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm what makes a synthetic control credible.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Synthetic control is most useful when: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1a" value="a"><label for="m9q1a">You have hundreds of treated units.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1b" value="b"><label for="m9q1b">A single unit is treated and no obvious comparison exists.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1c" value="c"><label for="m9q1c">Treatment is randomly assigned.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1d" value="d"><label for="m9q1d">There is a sharp cutoff.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Inference in synthetic control typically relies on: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2a" value="a"><label for="m9q2a">Clustered standard errors.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2b" value="b"><label for="m9q2b">A first-stage F-statistic.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2c" value="c"><label for="m9q2c">Placebo runs treating each donor as if it were treated.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2d" value="d"><label for="m9q2d">The McCrary test.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A poor pre-intervention fit means: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3a" value="a"><label for="m9q3a">The post-period gap cannot be interpreted as the effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3b" value="b"><label for="m9q3b">The effect is larger.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3c" value="c"><label for="m9q3c">The donor pool is too small only.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3d" value="d"><label for="m9q3d">Parallel trends holds.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>For a single-state or single-city reform you know, which other units would you put in the donor pool, and which would you exclude because they faced their own shock at the same time?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 10, 'Module 10: Causal Machine Learning',
  'Estimating heterogeneous effects without fishing, causal forests and double machine learning, and the firm line that ML supplies flexibility, not identification.',
  $html$<h2>Module 10: Causal Machine Learning</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">The toolkit so far estimates one average effect. Often the question is sharper: for whom does the programme work, and how do we target the next rupee? Machine learning, bent to causal questions, estimates effects that vary across people without overfitting them into noise.</p></div>
<h3>From Average to Heterogeneous Effects</h3>
<p>A cash transfer or an input subsidy rarely helps everyone equally. The conditional average treatment effect, CATE, is the effect as a function of characteristics: <em>E</em>[<em>Y</em>(1) &minus; <em>Y</em>(0) | <em>X</em> = <em>x</em>]. Estimating it by hand invites fishing: slice the data enough ways and a spurious subgroup effect always appears.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Two Tools That Discipline the Search</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Causal forests</div><div class="definition-text">Wager and Athey (2018) adapt random forests to estimate the CATE, with honest sample-splitting so the data used to find subgroups is not the data used to estimate their effects. This guards against the fishing problem.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Double machine learning</div><div class="definition-text">Chernozhukov et al. (2018). Use flexible learners for the nuisance parts (the outcome model and the treatment model), then combine them so the treatment-effect estimate stays valid and its confidence interval is honest, even with many covariates.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>These methods estimate heterogeneity; they do not create identification.</strong> A causal forest on observational data still needs unconfoundedness. The machine learning handles flexibility and many covariates. It does not rescue a design where the selection-bias term is non-zero.</p></div>
<p>The practitioner payoff is targeting. If a programme’s effect concentrates in a definable group, a causal forest can rank households by predicted benefit, and a budget can be aimed where it does the most good rather than spread evenly. That is the difference between an evaluation that scores a programme and one that improves it.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Honest splitting is the whole point.</strong> Finding subgroups and estimating their effects on the same data is the oldest way to manufacture a false positive. The value of causal forests is the discipline, not the flexibility. A subgroup result without out-of-sample validation is a hypothesis, not a finding.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana Soni" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana Soni</div><div class="coach-message">Funders increasingly ask "who benefits most" so they can target. Causal ML gives a credible answer, but only on a credible design. I am blunt with teams: a forest on a confounded comparison is a confounded answer with better graphics. Get identification right first, then let the machine find the heterogeneity.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<pre><code>## R: causal forest for heterogeneous effects and targeting
library(grf)
cf &lt;- causal_forest(X = covariates, Y = outcome, W = treat)
tau_hat &lt;- predict(cf)$predictions      # estimated CATE per household
best_linear_projection(cf, covariates)    # which covariates drive the effect</code></pre>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Wager &amp; Athey (2018), <em>JASA</em> 113(523); Athey &amp; Imbens (2016), <em>PNAS</em> 113(27); Chernozhukov et al. (2018), <em>Econometrics Journal</em> 21(1); Künzel et al. (2019), <em>PNAS</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Athey &amp; Imbens (2019). "Machine Learning Methods Economists Should Know About." <em>Annual Review of Economics</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Wager &amp; Athey (2018). "Estimation and Inference of Heterogeneous Treatment Effects using Random Forests."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Chernozhukov et al. (2018). "Double/Debiased Machine Learning."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm what causal ML does and does not provide.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The CATE is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1a" value="a"><label for="m10q1a">The average effect over everyone.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1b" value="b"><label for="m10q1b">The treatment effect as a function of covariates, E[Y(1)-Y(0)|X].</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1c" value="c"><label for="m10q1c">The first-stage coefficient.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1d" value="d"><label for="m10q1d">The propensity score.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Causal forests guard against false subgroup findings mainly through: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2a" value="a"><label for="m10q2a">Larger samples.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2b" value="b"><label for="m10q2b">Stronger instruments.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2c" value="c"><label for="m10q2c">Honest sample-splitting between finding and estimating subgroups.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2d" value="d"><label for="m10q2d">Parallel trends.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Applying a causal forest to a confounded observational comparison gives: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3a" value="a"><label for="m10q3a">A confounded answer, because ML does not supply identification.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3b" value="b"><label for="m10q3b">An unbiased ATE.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3c" value="c"><label for="m10q3c">A valid instrument.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3d" value="d"><label for="m10q3d">A sharp RD.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>If a funder asked you to target a programme to the households that gain most, what covariates might drive heterogeneity, and what design would you need before a causal forest’s ranking could be trusted?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 11, 'Module 11: Reading and Interrogating an Evaluation',
  'A deliberate reading order and standing checklist, specification stability and publication bias, and the credibility debate (Banerjee-Duflo against Deaton and Pritchett) stated without false balance.',
  $html$<h2>Module 11: Reading and Interrogating an Evaluation</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Most practitioners will commission and read far more impact studies than they will ever run. This module is about the harder skill: holding a finished evaluation up to the light and deciding how much weight it can bear.</p></div>
<h3>The Reading Order</h3>
<p>Read an impact evaluation in a deliberate sequence, not front to back. First the estimand: what effect, on whom. Then the identification strategy and its assumption, stated in one sentence. Then the threats specific to that design, from the relevant module. Only then the results table, and last the abstract, which is where authors are most generous to themselves.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> A Standing Checklist</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Identification</div><div class="definition-text">Which design, which assumption, and is the assumption argued or merely asserted.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The specific threat</div><div class="definition-text">RCT: compliance, attrition, spillovers. IV: exclusion, first-stage strength. RD: manipulation. DiD: pre-trends and staggered timing.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Specification stability</div><div class="definition-text">Does the result survive sensible alternative specifications, or only one. A specification curve (Simonsohn, Simmons &amp; Nelson 2020) shows the whole distribution rather than the author’s favourite point.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Generalisation</div><div class="definition-text">To what population and setting does the number plausibly travel, and on what argument.</div></div></div>
<h3>The Credibility Debate, Without the False Balance</h3>
<p>The randomised-evaluation movement, associated with Banerjee and Duflo (2011) and recognised by the 2019 Nobel, sharpened development economics by insisting on credible identification. Deaton (2010, <em>JEL</em>) and Pritchett and Sandefur (2015) pressed the obvious limit: an internally valid estimate from one trial does not tell you what the programme will do elsewhere, and mechanically transporting RCT results across contexts can mislead more than a context-specific observational study.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> My read</div><p>The critics are right about external validity and wrong if taken to dismiss the method. The discipline of stating an identifying assumption is a permanent gain, and the field is better for it. The honest position is narrow internal claims plus an explicit, falsifiable argument about transport, rather than either RCT triumphalism or a retreat to unidentified storytelling.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Publication bias is structural, not occasional.</strong> Significant, surprising results are likelier to be published (Andrews &amp; Kasy 2019). A single striking finding, especially an early one, is weaker than it looks until it replicates. Weight a literature, not a paper.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">When I review an evaluation for a board, I write the identifying assumption in one sentence at the top of my note, then ask whether the paper earns it. If the assumption needs a paragraph of hedging, that is the finding. Most of my value to a funder is killing the over-claim before it reaches a decision.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Deaton (2010), <em>JEL</em> 48(2); Banerjee &amp; Duflo (2009, 2011); Pritchett &amp; Sandefur (2015), <em>J. Development Studies</em>; Andrews &amp; Kasy (2019), <em>AER</em>; Simonsohn, Simmons &amp; Nelson (2020), <em>Nature Human Behaviour</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Deaton, A. (2010). "Instruments, Randomization, and Learning about Development." <em>JEL</em> 48(2).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Pritchett &amp; Sandefur (2015). "Learning from Experiments When Context Matters."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Banerjee &amp; Duflo (2009). "The Experimental Approach to Development Economics."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm how to read a study and where the credibility debate actually lands.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> When reading an impact evaluation, you should first establish: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1a" value="a"><label for="m11q1a">The abstract’s headline.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1b" value="b"><label for="m11q1b">The estimand and the identifying assumption.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1c" value="c"><label for="m11q1c">The journal’s ranking.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1d" value="d"><label for="m11q1d">The sample size.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> The external-validity critique of RCTs (Deaton; Pritchett &amp; Sandefur) holds that: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2a" value="a"><label for="m11q2a">An internally valid effect from one trial need not transport to other contexts.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2b" value="b"><label for="m11q2b">RCTs are never internally valid.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2c" value="c"><label for="m11q2c">Observational methods are always better.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2d" value="d"><label for="m11q2d">Randomisation introduces selection bias.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A specification curve is used to: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3a" value="a"><label for="m11q3a">Test the first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3b" value="b"><label for="m11q3b">Match treated and control units.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3c" value="c"><label for="m11q3c">Show the distribution of estimates across reasonable specifications, not one chosen result.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3d" value="d"><label for="m11q3d">Build a synthetic control.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take an evaluation you have cited in your own work. Write its identifying assumption in one sentence. Does the study earn that assumption, and how far would you let the result travel?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 12, 'Module 12: When There Is No Counterfactual',
  'Credible evaluation without a comparison group: contribution analysis, process tracing, realist CMO, and QCA, and the standard of stating what would disconfirm the claim.',
  $html$<h2>Module 12: When There Is No Counterfactual</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Much of development work cannot be evaluated with a control group. Advocacy, institutional reform, network building, and one-off national programmes have no comparison units and no clean before-and-after. Refusing to evaluate these is not an option. This module is the credible toolkit for the no-counterfactual world.</p></div>
<h3>Theory-Based Evaluation</h3>
<p>When you cannot estimate a counterfactual, you test the causal story instead. Lay out the theory of change as a chain of claims, then gather evidence for each link and, harder, against plausible rival explanations. The credibility comes from the rigour of the testing, not from a comparison group.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z"/></svg> Four Credible Approaches</div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Contribution analysis</div><div class="definition-text">Mayne (2008). Build the results chain, assemble evidence for each link, and seriously assess other factors that could explain the outcome. The deliverable is a contribution story that survives scrutiny, not an effect size.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Process tracing</div><div class="definition-text">Collier (2011). Use within-case evidence as diagnostic tests. A hoop test must be passed for a claim to survive; a smoking-gun test, if passed, strongly confirms it. Bayesian process tracing updates belief on each piece of evidence.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Realist evaluation</div><div class="definition-text">Pawson &amp; Tilley (1997). Ask what works, for whom, in what circumstances, through the context-mechanism-outcome configuration. The question is the mechanism, not the average.</div></div><div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Qualitative comparative analysis</div><div class="definition-text">Ragin (1987). Across a medium number of cases, identify which combinations of conditions are necessary or sufficient for the outcome, using set theory rather than correlation.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> Key Idea</div><p><strong>No counterfactual is not the same as no rigour.</strong> A well-executed contribution analysis or process trace can be more credible than a badly identified quasi-experiment. The standard is the same: state the claim, state what would disconfirm it, and go looking for the disconfirming evidence honestly.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert_triangle.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The misuse to avoid.</strong> These methods are sometimes chosen not because a counterfactual is impossible but because it is inconvenient. That is a problem. Reserve theory-based designs for cases where a counterfactual genuinely cannot exist, and say plainly when you are using them as a fallback rather than a first choice.</p></div></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/vandana-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Vandana Soni" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Vandana Soni</div><div class="coach-message">When a funder wants impact numbers for an advocacy programme, I do not invent a counterfactual that cannot exist. I offer a contribution analysis with explicit rival explanations and a process trace of the key decisions. Most funders respect the honesty once they see the alternative is a fabricated comparison.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<p>These approaches connect back to the MEL flagship’s distinction between attribution and contribution. Modules 1 to 10 are about attribution, isolating a programme’s effect against a counterfactual. This module is about contribution, defensible reasoning when isolation is impossible.</p>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Mayne (2008), ILAC Brief 16; Collier (2011), <em>PS: Political Science &amp; Politics</em> 44(4); Pawson &amp; Tilley (1997), <em>Realistic Evaluation</em>; Ragin (1987), <em>The Comparative Method</em>; White &amp; Phillips (2012), 3ie Working Paper.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Mayne, J. (2008). "Contribution Analysis: An Approach to Exploring Cause and Effect."</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Collier, D. (2011). "Understanding Process Tracing." <em>PS</em> 44(4).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Befani &amp; Stedman-Bryce (2017). "Process Tracing and Bayesian Updating for Impact Evaluation." <em>Evaluation</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Confirm you can match a no-counterfactual method to its logic.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Contribution analysis (Mayne 2008) builds credibility by: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1a" value="a"><label for="m12q1a">Randomising participants.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1b" value="b"><label for="m12q1b">Assembling evidence for each link in the results chain and assessing rival explanations.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1c" value="c"><label for="m12q1c">Matching on the propensity score.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1d" value="d"><label for="m12q1d">Plotting parallel trends.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> In process tracing, a "smoking-gun" test: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2a" value="a"><label for="m12q2a">Strongly confirms a claim if the evidence is found.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2b" value="b"><label for="m12q2b">Must be passed for a claim to survive.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2c" value="c"><label for="m12q2c">Tests the first stage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2d" value="d"><label for="m12q2d">Checks covariate balance.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Theory-based evaluation is the appropriate choice when: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3a" value="a"><label for="m12q3a">A control group is available but inconvenient.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3b" value="b"><label for="m12q3b">You want a precise effect size.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3c" value="c"><label for="m12q3c">A counterfactual genuinely cannot be constructed, as in national advocacy.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3d" value="d"><label for="m12q3d">Treatment is randomly assigned.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Name a programme in your portfolio that has no possible counterfactual. Which of the four approaches fits it best, and what single piece of evidence would most strongly disconfirm its main causal claim?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('causal', 13, 'Module 13: Capstone Project',
  'Design and defend a credible evaluation for a real causal claim: estimand, design, identifying assumption, threats and checks, data and code, honest limits, and a one-page brief.',
  $html$<h2>Module 13: Capstone Project</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Pick a real causal claim, one you might actually need to defend to a funder or a board, and build the case for the design that can bear it. The capstone is not an exam. It is the deliverable this whole course exists to make you capable of producing.</p></div>
<h3>The Brief</h3>
<p>Choose a programme or policy and a specific causal claim about it. State the estimand precisely, name the design you would use to recover it, defend the identifying assumption, list the threats and how you would check each, and say honestly what the evidence could and could not establish. If no counterfactual is possible, build the theory-based case from Module 12 instead and say why.</p>
<div class="capstone-box"><div class="capstone-header"><div class="capstone-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 19v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2zm0 0V9a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v10m-6 0a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v14a2 2 0 01-2 2h-2a2 2 0 01-2-2z"/></svg></div><h4 class="capstone-title">Design and Defend a Credible Evaluation</h4></div><div class="capstone-timeline"><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">1</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Claim and estimand</h5><p>State the causal question in one sentence and the estimand in potential-outcomes terms. Is it an ATT, an ATE, a LATE, a local effect at a cutoff? Justify which one the decision actually needs.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">2</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Design and identifying assumption</h5><p>Choose the design from Modules 4 to 10, or the theory-based route from Module 12. Write the identifying assumption in one sentence and defend it. Draw the DAG.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">3</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Threats and checks</h5><p>List the threats specific to your design and the diagnostic for each: balance, first-stage F, McCrary test, pre-trends and event study, placebo runs, stability across specifications.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">4</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Data and code plan</h5><p>Specify the data you need, the unit and level of analysis, the sample size or power, and the estimator. Sketch the R or Stata you would run.</p></div></div><div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">5</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Honest limits and a one-page brief</h5><p>State what the result could and could not establish, and how far it would travel. Distil the whole thing into a one-page brief a non-technical decision-maker could act on.</p></div></div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg> What good looks like</div><p>A strong capstone is honest about what the design cannot do. The weakest submissions over-claim. The strongest ones name the single assumption the whole estimate rests on, show how they would stress it, and tell the decision-maker plainly where the evidence stops. That judgement, not the cleverness of the method, is the mark of a practitioner who can be trusted with a research budget.</p></div>
<div class="coach-callout"><img src="https://www.impactmojo.in/assets/images/varna-photo.jpg" alt="Coach Varna" class="coach-photo"><div class="coach-content"><div class="coach-name">Varna</div><div class="coach-message">Bring your capstone to a coaching session and I will play the sceptical funder. We will find the weakest link in the identification together, before a real reviewer does. That single conversation has saved more proposals than any reading I could assign.</div><div class="coach-links"><a href="/coaching" class="coach-link"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Chat.svg" alt=""> Book 1:1 Coaching</a><a href="/#workshops" class="coach-link secondary"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flare.svg" alt=""> Join a Workshop</a></div></div></div>
<p>When you finish, you will have done the thing this course is for: taken a claim someone wanted to make, and decided, with reasons you can defend, exactly how much weight it can carry.</p>
<div class="source-citation">Sources: Synthesises Modules 1 to 12. Core references: Imbens &amp; Rubin (2015); Angrist &amp; Pischke (2009); Cunningham (2021), <em>Causal Inference: The Mixtape</em>.</div>
<div class="reading-box"><h4><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Book.svg" alt="" class="section-header-icon"> Essential Reading</h4><ul class="reading-list"><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Cunningham (2021). <em>Causal Inference: The Mixtape</em> (free online).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Gertler et al. (2016). <em>Impact Evaluation in Practice</em>, 2nd ed. (World Bank).</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="#" target="_blank"><span>Glennerster &amp; Takavarasha (2013). <em>Running Randomized Evaluations</em>.</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="external-icon" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"/></svg></a></li></ul></div>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">A short self-check on the capstone standard before you submit.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> The single most important sentence in your capstone is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1a" value="a"><label for="m13q1a">The literature review.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1b" value="b"><label for="m13q1b">The identifying assumption, stated plainly and defended.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1c" value="c"><label for="m13q1c">The sample size.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1d" value="d"><label for="m13q1d">The abstract.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A strong capstone is distinguished mainly by: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2a" value="a"><label for="m13q2a">Honesty about what the design cannot establish.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2b" value="b"><label for="m13q2b">The most advanced estimator available.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2c" value="c"><label for="m13q2c">The largest dataset.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2d" value="d"><label for="m13q2d">The most citations.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> If no counterfactual is possible for your chosen programme, you should: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3a" value="a"><label for="m13q3a">Abandon the evaluation.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3b" value="b"><label for="m13q3b">Invent a comparison group anyway.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3c" value="c"><label for="m13q3c">Build a theory-based case (contribution analysis or process tracing) and say why.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3d" value="d"><label for="m13q3d">Use a causal forest.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Before you start, write the one-sentence causal claim and the one-sentence identifying assumption your capstone will defend. If you cannot write the second sentence cleanly, that is the part to work on first.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false);
